================================================================================
TEST T44969 -- CLI show mirror interface direction set to both
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09 09:18-09:33 NZ (box local; both device clocks set to box local time
          before the run, so device and host stamps are directly comparable)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2, u3, u5; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWP-5318, objective "set a port mirror's direction to both and confirm, from the
          CLI and on the wire, that traffic in both directions is copied", 4 steps
Build:    swi_c  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (x960-tb277.rel, build Mon Sep 21 11:25:03 UTC 2026)
          swi_b  sin_x96056-20260921-9
Configs:  swi_c.cfg, swi_b.cfg (running-config after setup; saved to startup: yes, for the
          persistence step)

VERDICT: FAIL -- `mirror interface port1.0.48 direction both` is accepted, reported correctly
         by every CLI status command and survives a save and reload, but copies NO frames at
         all to the mirror destination.
      command accepted .................. yes      (no error of any kind)
      show mirror ....................... correct  (dest port1.0.10, both, src port1.0.48)
      show mirror interface <src> ....... correct
      running-config .................... present
      survives save and reload .......... yes      (configuration intact after reboot)
      ingress frames copied ............. 0        (6 expected)
      egress frames copied .............. 0        (6 expected)
      transmit on the same boot ......... 6 frames (works)
      receive on the same boot .......... 6 frames (works)
FAIL CONDITION: with `direction both` configured on the destination port, a 6-ping flow
         across the monitored port must deliver BOTH directions to the mirror destination --
         6 echo requests (what the DUT receives on port1.0.48) AND 6 echo replies (what it
         transmits out of it), 12 frames in total.
           expected : 6 echo requests AND 6 echo replies at tb277 eth3
           observed : 0 and 0, in five separate runs, tcpdump reporting
                      "0 packets received by filter" every time
         The ping passed 6/6 at 0% loss in every one of those runs, so the traffic to be
         mirrored was present on the monitored port throughout.
         WHY THIS IS THE PRODUCT AND NOT THE METHOD: `direction transmit` and `direction
         receive` were applied on the SAME boot, the SAME cabling, the SAME 6-ping flow and
         by the SAME procedure, and each delivered exactly its expected frames -- transmit 6
         replies and 0 requests, receive 6 requests and 0 replies. A method, bench or capture
         fault would have taken those two down with it. The decisive run was on a PRISTINE
         BOOT: `both` was saved to startup-config and the DUT reloaded, so the hardware was
         programmed from flash with no re-application history at all, and it still copied
         nothing.
TRIED:   re-measured with no configuration change, in case the mirror needed settle time
           -> nothing copied
         bounced the destination port a second time, with 25 s settle
           -> nothing copied
         checked the link at both ends during the failure
           -> DUT `connected ... a-full a-1000`; host NIC `UP ... LOWER_UP`
         checked it was not a capture-side fault
           -> tcpdump reported "0 packets RECEIVED BY FILTER", so nothing reached eth3 at
              all, and an unfiltered capture caught not one frame of any kind in 14 s
         checked the mirror engine was not wedged by the preceding reconfigurations
           -> re-applied `direction transmit` mid-session: 6 echo replies captured. The
              engine was working seconds before and after `both` failed.
         saved, reloaded, and re-tested on the pristine boot, then re-tested both working
         directions on that same boot
           -> `both` still nothing; transmit and receive both correct
CAVEAT:  This is a dataplane fault, not a configuration fault: the CLI, the status commands
         and the saved configuration are all correct and consistent. There is no error, no
         log message and no CLI indication that anything is wrong. Only a frame capture
         reveals it. Two further defects found elsewhere in this group -- a re-applied mirror
         needing a destination-port bounce, and an in-place direction change being silently
         ignored -- mean mirror status output on this platform cannot be read as a statement
         about the dataplane at all.

