STEP 1 -- "set the mirror direction to both and read it back"             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface port1.0.48 direction both
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: both                    <-- direction as set
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ#show mirror interface port1.0.48
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: both
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
   mirror interface port1.0.48 direction both
  => STEP 1 PASS: the CLI accepts `direction both` and both show forms and the running-config
     report it. The configuration half of the case is correct.

STEP 2 -- "confirm on the wire that both directions are copied"           FAIL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the destination port was bounced after applying the mirror, which is what makes a
  re-applied mirror take effect on this platform. Expected 12 frames -- 6 requests and 6
  replies.
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n 'icmp'  while pinging 10.38.22.70 from 10.38.22.65
  0 packets captured
  0 packets received by filter
  0 packets dropped by kernel
host# ping -c 6 -i 0.5 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  counted: echo REQUESTS = 0 ; echo REPLIES = 0
  => STEP 2 FAIL: nothing at all was copied, while the identical flow passed end to end.

